1
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:12,877
Eleven fifty-nine. The city is asleep. Your phone isn’t.

2
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:23,150
At midnight, it updates, like it always does.

3
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:27,089
Except tonight, the news is dated tomorrow.

4
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:33,513
Most of it is ordinary. The weather. The trams. A lost dog.

5
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:36,976
You laugh, and go to sleep.

6
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:51,955
Seven forty-one. The sky is dry.

7
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:54,556
Seven forty-two.

8
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:07,650
Real forecasters can’t do this. Even with supercomputers, their forecasts gain only about one day of accuracy every ten years.

9
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:11,822
Yours is perfect. Down to the minute.

10
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:20,795
Every headline comes true. The late tram. The lost dog on Mill Street. The final score.

11
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:28,623
For one whole morning, you know everything a day early.

12
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:44,491
Then, at noon, a new headline.

13
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:53,055
YOU: Rosa. Your heater. Unplug it. Please.

14
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:58,076
ROSA: That old thing? It’s kept me warm for twenty years.

15
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:02,732
YOU: Not past ten past two.

16
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:06,132
ROSA: All right. For you.

17
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:17,623
Ten past two. Nothing burns.

18
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:23,294
And on your screen, the headline rewrites itself.

19
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:37,494
In 1961, the meteorologist Edward Lorenz re-ran a weather simulation, but typed in one number rounded off.

20
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:43,428
0.506, instead of 0.506127.

21
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:49,956
Within two simulated months, the forecast described a completely different sky.

22
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:56,833
Tomorrow is not written in stone. It bends to the smallest touch. Even yours.

23
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:01,850
That night, the worst headline yet.

24
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:08,275
Northgate Savings Bank collapses. Thousands of families lose everything they have saved.

25
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:12,492
So you warn them.

26
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:22,751
Ten shares. Then a hundred. Then a thousand.

27
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:31,843
By three in the morning, half the city has read your warning.

28
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:49,760
At dawn, they come to take their money out. All of them. At once.

29
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:12,050
In 1948, the sociologist Robert Merton gave this a name: the self-fulfilling prophecy.

30
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:15,768
A false belief that makes itself come true.

31
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:23,150
A bank never keeps all its savers’ money in cash. If everyone asks on the same day, it cannot pay.

32
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:31,050
In March 2023, customers of one American bank tried to withdraw $42 billion in a single day.

33
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:34,584
By the next morning, its doors were closed.

34
00:04:46,150 --> 00:04:49,157
The bank was never going to fail.

35
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:52,676
Until you told everyone it would.

36
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:59,680
Two days later, the one headline you cannot ignore.

37
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:14,302
DISPATCH: Emergency services. What’s your emergency?

38
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:20,756
YOU: The Old River Bridge. It’s going to collapse at 7:04. Close it!

39
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:25,628
DISPATCH: Wait. You’re the courier. The one from the bank post.

40
00:05:27,350 --> 00:05:32,463
DISPATCH: Do you know how many people lost their jobs this week? Get off this line.

41
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:45,499
The Greeks told of Cassandra, a princess who could see the future, cursed so that no one would ever believe her.

42
00:05:46,650 --> 00:05:49,588
Tonight, that’s you.

43
00:05:51,650 --> 00:05:56,172
Twenty-four minutes. Nine percent battery.

44
00:06:32,650 --> 00:06:36,364
Nobody believes a warning from a stranger.

45
00:06:37,150 --> 00:06:41,535
But almost everyone stops for a person standing in the road.

46
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:48,979
YOU: Stay back! Please! Stay back!

47
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:04,297
Seven oh three.

48
00:07:27,650 --> 00:07:32,279
DRIVER: You were standing right in the road. You stopped all of us.

49
00:07:41,650 --> 00:07:46,504
In the morning, your phone shows today’s news. Only today’s.

50
00:07:53,650 --> 00:07:56,937
Nobody gets to read tomorrow.

51
00:07:57,150 --> 00:08:00,099
But everybody writes it.
